othing appended; the stream stays clean.\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:247:is a library duty wrapping `dao.space.transactor`'s `:transactor` stream\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:291:`(schema/transactor local-stream intake-pool {:strict true})`. The default\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:382:reopen is required. Ownership is explicit: `schema/transactor`\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:631:(def log (schema/transactor local-stream intake-pool))\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:78:  `dao.stream` is its own transactor: it writes atomic transaction records to its local\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:79:  stream, with no global contention and no commit step. It also carries the transactor's\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:392:   [`dao.space.transactor`](dao.space.transactor.md)). The local stream is\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:431:- **Write recovery:** A restarted writer reopens its file in append mode; the next `ds/append!`\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:476:The worker loop below reads with `query/q` and writes with `ds/append!` — two different\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:579:own — each claim's `t` is a per-local-stream transaction counter (`dao.space.transactor`\ndocs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md:39:- **No new mechanism is required, on either side.** `dao.space.transactor/DaoStreamLog`\ndocs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md:108:## Amendment (2026-09-09): the dao.stream v2 swap reached the transactor\ndocs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md:113:- `dao.space.transactor/DaoStreamLog` no longer exists, and the deposit target\ndocs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md:130:  `dao.stream.md`, *Complete history*, and `dao.space.transactor.md` (T18):\ndocs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:4:`:transactor` DaoStream type it replaces is deleted). This document is the\ndocs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:14:  transactor; the snapshot reads this namespace's local stream\ndocs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:21:## A transactor is a value, not a stream\ndocs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:23:A transactor is an **interpreter over a stream, not a stream**\ndocs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:26:stream contract for either. The v1 `:transactor` DaoStream type — a\ndocs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:38:(def log (transactor/create! {:local-stream local   ; reader+writer surface;\ndocs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:45:(transactor/close!    log)        ; → {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}\ndocs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:50:following `query/open-published!`. A transactor implements no dao.stream\ndocs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:97:| transactor itself closed | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` |\ndocs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:5:coordinator. Stigmergy is writing datoms to the agent's own `dao.stream` via `ds/append!` and\ndocs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:134:coordinate two writers over the same local stream. One transactor per local\ndocs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:246:   transactor value with that stream plus an explicit DaoJing intake pool\ndocs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:248:   through `transactor/append!`/`transact!`, using integer stream-local entity ids; the\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:15:  Transactor/Storage/Query onto streams\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:28:the transactor: every agent appending to its own `dao.stream` is its own\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:106:- **The transactor entry point** — `publish-index!`: snapshot the stream,\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:218:created by `transactor/create!` over a spec, not a stream and not a\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:219:descriptor (see `dao.space.transactor.md`, and `dao.space.md`, *The Write\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:237:(def log (transactor/create! {:local-stream local\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:255:`dao.space.transactor.md`, T18). One wrapper per local stream is a hard\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.md | sed -n '420,485p'\nnl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '1,15p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.md | sed -n '20,35p'\ngit show HEAD:docs/design/dao.space.schema.md | sed -n '244,251p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.md | sed -n '420,485p'\nnl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '1,15p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.md | sed -n '20,35p'\ngit show HEAD:docs/design/dao.space.schema.md | sed -n '244,251p'\"","aggregated_output":"   420\tagent-transactor loop*.\n   421\t\n   422\t### Fault Tolerance (Crash-Only Semantics)\n   423\t\n   424\tBecause the write path uses persistent append-only `dao.stream` files, the space inherits\n   425\tcrash-only semantics natively:\n   426\t\n   427\t- **Data safety:** Datoms flushed before a crash are safe; append-only files have no\n   428\t  partial-update corruption window.\n   429\t- **Reader behavior:** A reader tailing a crashed writer's stream simply reaches the end and\n   430\t  yields (`ds/next` returns `:blocked`); it waits for new data rather than failing.\n   431\t- **Write recovery:** A restarted writer reopens its file in append mode; the next `ds/append!`\n   432\t  lands safely after the last flushed datom.\n   433\t- **Read recovery:** A reader resumes from a checkpointed cursor, so an incremental index\n   434\t  rebuilds without reprocessing or skipping. A checkpoint persists the whole cursor map\n   435\t  (`(:cursor result)`, not just a bare `:position` offset when the transport's cursors carry\n   436\t  more); a cursor that has fallen behind a retention boundary returns `:daostream/gap`, and\n   437\t  resynchronization is the caller's decision. Because `publish-index!` never rewrites the\n   438\t  local stream — it appends to a separate intake stream — publishing cannot reorder what a\n   439\t  local-stream cursor walks.\n   440\t\n   441\t## Coordination: Stigmergy\n   442\t\n   443\tAgents coordinate by leaving datoms in `dao.jing` for others to query, decoupled in time and\n   444\tidentity.\n   445\t\n   446\t**An interpreter taps a stream without being aware of its source.** This is the property that\n   447\tmakes the coordination stigmergic rather than merely asynchronous, and it is the test to apply\n   448\twhen a design looks decoupled but is not. If an interpreter must be *handed* a particular\n   449\tproducer's stream, then something knows both sides and wired them together; the coupling has\n   450\tmoved from a function call to a reference, and has not been removed. The ant is not handed a\n   451\tpheromone trail. It encounters the medium and finds what is there.\n   452\t\n   453\tTwo consequences follow, and both are invariants:\n   454\t\n   455\t- **Nothing registers.** An interested interpreter holds a cursor and matches what it reads;\n   456\t  it does not declare interest and wait to be called. Registration is a subscription registry,\n   457\t  which is a callback table under a different name, and it reintroduces exactly the coupling\n   458\t  the medium exists to remove.\n   459\t- **A new interpreter starts reading without anything being rewired.** No producer is\n   460\t  modified, no wiring step is added, and no existing reader is disturbed. If adding a reader\n   461\t  requires touching a writer, the medium is not doing its job.\n   462\t\n   463\tBecause interpreters both read the medium and deposit back into it, refinement accretes in\n   464\tone place rather than travelling along wires. A host boundary deposits syntax; an interpreter\n   465\treads it and deposits the semantic fact it derived; a further interpreter reads that. This is\n   466\tthe blackboard working as designed -- successive levels of abstraction over one medium, with\n   467\tno participant aware of any other.\n   468\t\n   469\t`dao.space` is that medium. A separate system event bus would be a second stigmergic medium\n   470\tbeside the one this document describes; see\n   471\t[ADR 0003](adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md). Because streams are append-only there is no destructive `take`: to \"claim\" work an\n   472\tagent *appends a new datom* asserting the claim, and \"current state\" is a read-side query over\n   473\tthe accreted datoms. This is the tuple space working as designed — coordination with no\n   474\tbroker, no message-format negotiation, and no leader election.\n   475\t\n   476\tThe worker loop below reads with `query/q` and writes with `ds/append!` — two different\n   477\tconcurrency models. `append!` is a local append to the agent's own single-writer stream (one\n   478\tatomic transaction record); it is never a CAS over a shared surface. Visibility to other\n   479\tworkers requires publication: `publish!` enqueues the indexes, a DaoJing observer\n   480\tmaterializes them, and a reader folding the new manifest address sees the claim. `q` folds\n   481\tthe sources it is given fresh on each call, so a claim becomes visible to *other* workers\n   482\tonly once they re-query after republish — there is no push, no shared read-your-writes\n   483\tguarantee across agents.\n   484\t\n   485\t**The example below is representative, not a shipped binary.** Every mechanism it names is\n     1\t# DaoSpace for Agent Collaboration\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design discussion (2026-07-10); a coordinator-mediated prototype ran live the same\n     4\tday, then was **superseded (2026-07-12) by the streams-native model**: there is no\n     5\tcoordinator. Stigmergy is writing datoms to the agent's own `dao.stream` via `ds/append!` and\n     6\treading dao.space with `q`/`match` — nothing else. The living contract is\n     7\t`test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj`: agents coordinate over a network-accessible\n     8\t`dao.jing.file` content handle (served with `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers` as\n     9\t`:jing/put-content` and `:jing/get-content` RPC operations), and the finished space persists at\n    10\t`target/stigmergy-space.db` for inspection with `dao.space.query`.\n    11\tDescribes how `dao.space` serves as a coordination medium for autonomous agents — LLM agents\n    12\tspecifically — and enumerates what exists today versus what is still needed. Nothing here\n    13\tproposes changing the tuple-space model; the model is the point.\n    14\t\n    15\t**Related documents:**\n    20\t- `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` — the cross-platform tree and its storage\n    21\t  adapter\n    22\t- `docs/datomic.md` — the architecture the analogy below leans on\n    23\t\n    24\t## Why a separate library\n    25\t\n    26\tIn Datomic, the **transactor** builds the covered indexes and saves them to\n    27\tstorage; **peers** pull segments and answer queries. datom.world decentralizes\n    28\tthe transactor: every agent appending to its own `dao.stream` is its own\n    29\ttransactor (`dao.space.md`, *Three Boundaries*) — so every agent also owns the\n    30\ttransactor's other duty, **indexing its own datoms**. That duty needs a\n    31\tlibrary the same way querying does:\n    32\t\n    33\t- `dao.space.index` — what a stream owner runs: snapshot the local stream,\n    34\t  build the covered indexes, and enqueue them as immutable content-addressed\n    35\t  segments plus a manifest through a DaoJing intake stream. The write-side\n## 3. The write boundary: a validating wrapper\n\nDatomic's schema is enforced by its transactor. Here the schema interpreter\nis a library duty wrapping `dao.space.transactor`'s `:transactor` stream\nwrapper — the same layering `yin.vm` already demonstrates with its own\nDatomic-style schema map and `datoms->tx-data`\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`, `schema`): a client above the space declaring and\nenforcing a vocabulary at its own write boundary.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
