{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S GMT'; TZ=Asia/Bangkok date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %z (Asia/Bangkok)'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.md | sed -n '176,184p;387,440p;472,480p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.schema.md | sed -n '243,251p'; nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '1,8p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md | sed -n '82,106p'\"","aggregated_output":"2026-09-09 12:30:39 GMT\n2026-09-09 19:30:39 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)\n   176\t;; q — Datalog over the supplied source pool. The design contract is full Datalog\n   177\t;; (joins, negation, disjunction, aggregation, predicates, recursion); the\n   178\t;; implementation covers it: not/not-join, or/or-join, :find aggregates (count,\n   179\t;; count-distinct, sum, min, max, avg) with :with, predicate/function\n   180\t;; clauses via a caller-supplied {:fns {sym fn}} option, and recursive\n   181\t;; rules bound to % via :in (Datomic syntax; multiple bodies = OR,\n   182\t;; terminates on cyclic data). q returns a closed bounded result DaoStream.\n   183\t;; query/collect materializes conventional Datalog shapes.\n   184\t(query/collect\n   387\tTransactor. Indexing has two stages, mirroring Datomic's memory-index → disk-index pipeline\n   388\tbut without a central transactor process:\n   389\t\n   390\t1. **Append** — the agent's writes land in its own local `dao.stream`\n   391\t   memory-log as atomic transaction records (see\n   392\t   [`dao.space.transactor`](dao.space.transactor.md)). The local stream is\n   393\t   authoritative for the logical stream's process lifetime — the watermark and\n   394\t   the published indexes are derived from it — but it is not durable: a\n   395\t   restarted process sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity.\n   396\t   **The durable record is what publication puts in `dao.jing`**, and\n   397\t   un-published writes are not durable — which was equally true before the\n   398\t   v2 migration, when local streams were in-memory ring buffers. The\n   399\t   pipeline mirrors Datomic's memory-index → disk-index, and a memory index\n   400\t   is not durable either. A durable *stream* transport is not the fix: it\n   401\t   would put a second durable record beside the content store, which is\n   402\t   exactly the `dao.stream`/`dao.jing` unification\n   403\t   [`dao.space.query.md`](dao.space.query.md)'s *Decisions* ruled out (\"the\n   404\t   source stays a convention layered over the dumb content store … unifying\n   405\t   them would undo that\"). No storage handle is touched at append.\n   406\t2. **Publish** — when the agent runs\n   407\t   [`dao.space.index/publish-index!`](dao.space.index.md), the local stream is snapshotted,\n   408\t   the four covered indexes are built as immutable, content-addressed `dao.data.btree`\n   409\t   segments, and the node blobs plus the manifest are appended to one intake stream\n   410\t   selected from an explicit pool. A DaoJing observer materializes them into content\n   411\t   storage; other agents consume the persisted covered indexes by naming the manifest\n   412\t   address with a published source. The datoms themselves were already readable by anyone\n   413\t   who folds the published manifest; publication changes access cost, not visibility.\n   414\t\n   415\tPublishing is an acceleration, never a semantic change for `q`/`match` — readers answer\n   416\tidentically over a published manifest or a raw datom source. `publish-index!` returns the\n   417\tmanifest and its address and never mutates DaoJing directly; append success means enqueued,\n   418\tnot yet materialized. A partial immutable node prefix on a full intake stream is retry-safe\n   419\tbecause the manifest is always appended last. See `dao.space.index.md`, *The\n   420\tagent-transactor loop*.\n   421\t\n   422\t### Fault Tolerance (Crash-Only Semantics)\n   423\t\n   424\tBecause the write path uses persistent append-only `dao.stream` files, the space inherits\n   425\tcrash-only semantics natively:\n   426\t\n   427\t- **Data safety:** Datoms flushed before a crash are safe; append-only files have no\n   428\t  partial-update corruption window.\n   429\t- **Reader behavior:** A reader tailing a crashed writer's stream simply reaches the end and\n   430\t  yields (`ds/next` returns `:blocked`); it waits for new data rather than failing.\n   431\t- **Write recovery:** A restarted writer reopens its file in append mode; the next `ds/append!`\n   432\t  lands safely after the last flushed datom.\n   433\t- **Read recovery:** A reader resumes from a checkpointed cursor, so an incremental index\n   434\t  rebuilds without reprocessing or skipping. A checkpoint persists the whole cursor map\n   435\t  (`(:cursor result)`, not just a bare `:position` offset when the transport's cursors carry\n   436\t  more); a cursor that has fallen behind a retention boundary returns `:daostream/gap`, and\n   437\t  resynchronization is the caller's decision. Because `publish-index!` never rewrites the\n   438\t  local stream — it appends to a separate intake stream — publishing cannot reorder what a\n   439\t  local-stream cursor walks.\n   440\t\n   472\tagent *appends a new datom* asserting the claim, and \"current state\" is a read-side query over\n   473\tthe accreted datoms. This is the tuple space working as designed — coordination with no\n   474\tbroker, no message-format negotiation, and no leader election.\n   475\t\n   476\tThe worker loop below reads with `query/q` and writes with `ds/append!` — two different\n   477\tconcurrency models. `append!` is a local append to the agent's own single-writer stream (one\n   478\tatomic transaction record); it is never a CAS over a shared surface. Visibility to other\n   479\tworkers requires publication: `publish!` enqueues the indexes, a DaoJing observer\n   480\tmaterializes them, and a reader folding the new manifest address sees the claim. `q` folds\n   243\t\n   244\t## 3. The write boundary: a validating wrapper\n   245\t\n   246\tDatomic's schema is enforced by its transactor. Here the schema interpreter\n   247\tis a library duty wrapping `dao.space.transactor`'s `:transactor` stream\n   248\twrapper — the same layering `yin.vm` already demonstrates with its own\n   249\tDatomic-style schema map and `datoms->tx-data`\n   250\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`, `schema`): a client above the space declaring and\n   251\tenforcing a vocabulary at its own write boundary.\n     1\t# DaoSpace for Agent Collaboration\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design discussion (2026-07-10); a coordinator-mediated prototype ran live the same\n     4\tday, then was **superseded (2026-07-12) by the streams-native model**: there is no\n     5\tcoordinator. Stigmergy is writing datoms to the agent's own `dao.stream` via `ds/append!` and\n     6\treading dao.space with `q`/`match` — nothing else. The living contract is\n     7\t`test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj`: agents coordinate over a network-accessible\n     8\t`dao.jing.file` content handle (served with `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers` as\n    82\t| T16 | Intake pool members are validated with v2 `stream/writer?`; a non-empty collection is required |\n    83\t| T18 | The local stream is on a transport declaring complete retention (below) |\n    84\t| T19 | `dao.space.schema` installs its next state only when the transactor answered ok (pinned in `schema_test`) |\n    85\t| T20 | Schema's v1 public results keep their v1 shape until schema's own plan — the D10 rule (`dao.space.schema.md`) |\n    86\t\n    87\t## Operational outcomes are data; argument defects throw\n    88\t\n    89\tThe write path validates the local append's answer with\n    90\t`stream/valid-outcome?` before interpreting it, and answers:\n    91\t\n    92\t| Case | v2 behaviour |\n    93\t|---|---|\n    94\t| local append ok | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms ds}`, watermark advances |\n    95\t| local append `full`/`closed`/`invalid-value`/`transport-error` | returned as data, watermark **unchanged**, same `t` still pending |\n    96\t| local append answers a non-outcome | folded to `:dao.stream/transport-error` with `:dao.stream/answer` retained |\n    97\t| transactor itself closed | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` |\n    98\t| malformed entity map / explicit datom `t` / empty `tx-data` / zero datoms | **still throws** — defects in the caller's own argument, detected before any stream is touched |\n    99\t| local append throws | propagates, watermark unchanged |\n   100\t\n   101\t`full` means \"not yet, retry\", so throwing on it was always wrong; the retry\n   102\tcontract is now stronger, not weaker: *the watermark advances if and only if\n   103\tthe local append answered `:dao.stream/ok`*, so retrying the identical call\n   104\tre-attempts the identical `t`. On a `memory-log` local stream the only\n   105\treachable non-ok outcome is `closed`, since the transport excludes `full`,\n   106\t`invalid-value` and `transport-error` — but `append!`/`transact!` are library\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-09 12:30:39 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-09 19:30:39 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)  \nCoding-Agent: codex  \nSession-ID: 01a080de-1a15-7d23-9a35-4106b127e4f0\n\n# Phase 2 review\n\n## P1 — The edited design documents still contradict the migrated implementation\n\n**Files and lines:**\n\n- `docs/design/dao.space.md:182`\n- `docs/design/dao.space.md:422-439`\n- `docs/design/dao.space.md:476-477`\n- `docs/design/dao.space.schema.md:246-248`\n- `docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:3-6`\n\n**Blocks the commit: yes.**\n\nThe new material correctly says the local stream is a process-lifetime `memory-log`, that durability begins with publication into `dao.jing`, and that the transactor is a value rather than a stream. Several surviving passages say the opposite:\n\n- The *Fault Tolerance* section says the write path uses persistent append-only files, flushed datoms survive crashes, writers reopen those files, reads use `ds/next`, and eviction appears as `:daostream/gap`. None describes the committed `memory-log` composition. It directly contradicts the new text at lines 390-405.\n- The worker-loop introduction still says it writes through `ds/append!`; the example below now uses `transactor/append!`.\n- The schema design still calls the inner object a registered `:transactor` stream wrapper.\n- The stigmergy document’s opening definition still says agents write through `ds/append!`.\n- Independently, `dao.space.md:182` still says `q` returns a closed result DaoStream, although query now returns a bounded query value. This is older query residue, but this phase is already modifying the same design document and should not leave its architectural summary false.\n\n**Concrete change:** rewrite the fault-tolerance section around the actual two-stage model:\n\n- un-published memory-log contents do not survive process failure;\n- published content in `dao.jing` is durable;\n- rebuilding after restart starts from published state or a future causality-carrying checkpoint;\n- remove the v1 `ds/next`, file-reopen, and `:daostream/gap` claims.\n\nThen replace the remaining `ds/append!`, `:transactor` stream-wrapper, and result-DaoStream descriptions with the current named operations and value terminology.\n\n## P2 — D3’s malformed-answer fold has no executable test\n\n**Files and lines:**\n\n- `src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:134-155`\n- `docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md:89-103`\n- `test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc:43-95,495-525`\n\n**Blocks the commit: no, independently.**\n\nThe implementation is correct: a result that fails `stream/valid-outcome?` becomes:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n :dao.stream/answer answer}\n```\n\nand the watermark remains unchanged. However, the tests cover `full` and a thrown append but never a returned malformed value. Consequently, the permanent outcome table and the full “advances iff ok” claim are not completely pinned.\n\n**Concrete change:** add a reader/writer double whose first append returns something such as `:boom` and whose retry delegates to the memory-log. Assert the exact folded result, including `:dao.stream/answer`, and assert that the retry commits at the original `t`.\n\n## Verified and cleared\n\n- **D3 outcome discipline:** Correct in every implementation branch. Only `:dao.stream/ok` advances `next-t`; every valid non-ok append result is returned unchanged; malformed returned values fold to `transport-error`; thrown exceptions propagate before any watermark update.\n- **D10/T19/T20:** Correct. Schema computes the proposed state before emission, installs it only after an inner `ok`, returns non-ok outcomes unchanged, and reconstructs the exact legacy success shape `{:result :ok :t … :datoms …}`. Existing successful-result assertions therefore remain valid by construction.\n- **Schema refusal test:** `failed-inner-append-leaves-wrapper-state-unchanged` proves real properties: the wrapper state is unchanged after `full`, the retry uses the same `t`, successful state installation occurs afterward, and the legacy schema result shape survives.\n- **Closedness bridge:** Sound. Schema transact, publish, and close use the same wrapper-state lock. Publish either completes before close or is rejected after close; it cannot cross the close boundary. `SchemaWrapper.close!` preserves `{:woke []}` and closes only its owned inner transactor.\n- **ThrowingAppendStream:** The fix is correct. Its retry now delegates the bound `val`; the old `_val`/`val` mismatch did append `clojure.core/val`. No equivalent mistake remains in the other migrated doubles: intentionally unused parameters stay unused, while all delegating doubles pass their actual bound value or packet.\n- **Index snapshot:** Both cursor minting and every `next` result are validated before interpretation. Incremental flattening preserves first-defect ordering and does not read beyond a malformed element. `blocked` and `end` terminate; every other well-formed outcome reaches the complete-retention precondition failure.\n- **Phase independence:** The Phase 3 residue is properly isolated. The remaining v1 operations in `index_test` and `stigmergy_test/sources`, and the published adapter in `index.cljc`, still compile and retain their old behavior. Nothing in Phase 2 requires Phase 3 to land.\n- **Portability:** The production changes introduce no JVM-only classes or operations into shared execution paths. Locking remains JVM-only with the existing default path for other hosts; the concurrency tests provide explicit non-JVM branches.\n- **Structural residue:** `transactor.cljc` has no v1 require, `DaoStreamLog`, or `defopen`; the stated `ds/` reductions match the Phase 2 boundary.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**Phase 2 is not ready to commit because the edited permanent documentation contains direct contradictions about durability and the transactor API.** The implementation itself is sound on static review. After correcting that P1, adding the malformed-answer test is recommended but is not independently blocking.\n\nThe still-running CLJD lane remains an external readiness gate; I did not rerun any suite."}}
